proc: own the superblock by the pid namespace's user namespace.

proc_mount() looked up the pid namespace's superblock with sget(), which
uses the caller's user namespace. The kernel mount created when a pid
namespace gets its first process runs in the forking parent, so when a
process creates a user and a pid namespace in the same clone() call, as
bubblewrap does, that superblock belongs to the parent's user namespace
and the child's own proc mount fails with EBUSY. WebKitGTK's sandbox, and
with it Epiphany, could not start.

Pass the pid namespace's owner explicitly, as mainline's mount_ns() does.
The permission check over the pid namespace is unchanged.

--- a/fs/proc/root.c
+++ b/fs/proc/root.c
@@ -121,7 +121,16 @@
 			return ERR_PTR(-EPERM);
 	}
 
-	sb = sget(fs_type, proc_test_super, proc_set_super, flags, ns);
+	/*
+	 * Like mainline's mount_ns(), the superblock belongs to the user
+	 * namespace owning the pid namespace. sget() used the caller's: the
+	 * kernel mount made when a pid namespace gets its first process runs
+	 * in the forking parent, so with CLONE_NEWUSER|CLONE_NEWPID in one
+	 * clone() (bubblewrap) the child's own proc mount found that
+	 * superblock owned by another user namespace and failed with EBUSY.
+	 */
+	sb = sget_userns(fs_type, proc_test_super, proc_set_super, flags,
+			 ns->user_ns, ns);
 	if (IS_ERR(sb))
 		return ERR_CAST(sb);
 
